{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>But Noah found grace.</b> To save even his sons and daughters — not because he was worthy of that, but by way of gracious favor the blessed El granted him this, as it says: וּשְׁלֹשֶׁת הָאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵלֶּה בְּתוֹכָהּ נֹחַ דָּנִיֵּאל וְאִיּוֹב חַי ה' אִם בָּנִים וְאִם בָּנוֹת יַצִּילוּ הֵמָּה לְבַדָּם יִנָּצֵלוּ (Ezekiel 14:14). This is because they did not teach their generation to know YHWH, as did Abraham, Moses, Samuel, and others, as the Sages of blessed memory said: עֵילָם וְהֵם דָּנִיֵּאל וַחֲבֵרָיו זָכְתָה לִלְמוֹד וְלֹא זָכְתָה לְלַמֵּד (Pesaḥim 87a). And so Noah: although he rebuked them for their corrupt deeds in matters of governance, he did not instruct them to know the blessed El and to walk in His ways, even though he himself was righteous and whole in thought and in deed. For indeed a righteous man who perfects himself alone is worthy of being saved alone, but one who also perfects others is worthy of saving others as well, for in this there is hope that he may restore them in repentance, as the Sages of blessed memory said (Berakhot 19a): אִם רָאִיתָ תַּלְמִיד חָכָם שֶׁסָּרַח בַּיּוֹם אַל תְּהַרְהֵר אַחֲרָיו בַּלַּיְלָה, שֶׁוַּדַּאי עָשָׂה תְּשׁוּבָה. End of the pericope of Genesis."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Sforno quotes the Talmudic phrase עֵילָם וְהֵם דָּנִיֵּאל וַחֲבֵרָיו verbatim; the cryptic עֵילָם is retained as he cites it. The Talmudic citation references Pesaḥim 87a and Berakhot 19a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:29:28.489630925Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}